      "id": 1,
      "text": "Laurel was platted in 1836 by James Conwell, a Maryland native, who named it after his hometown of Laurel, Maryland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurel%2C_Indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s post office opened in 1837, marking its growing importance in Franklin County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.in.gov/towns/laurel/about/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1902, Laurel became the last known shooting site of the wild passenger pigeon, the most abundant bird in North America.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurel%2C_Indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Whitewater Canal’s construction turned Laurel into a key shipping point, boosting local commerce in the 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.in.gov/towns/laurel/about/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A devastating fire in 1899, sparked by an overturned kerosene lamp, destroyed much of Market Street.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.in.gov/towns/laurel/about/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Laurel’s early economy featured a mix of grocers, dry goods stores, blacksmiths, wheelwrights, and even a carriage factory.",
